Latin'plumbum 'for Lead
The old name of Bismuth is'Wismut ', which stood for'white mass'.
Named after Poland to honor Marie Curie
Greek'astator 'for'changing'
Named after Radium. It ends with'on 'to make it clear that it is a noble gas.
Named after France to honor Marguerite Perey
Latin'radius' for'beam ', as it is radioactive
Greek'aktis' for'beam '- actinium is radioactive
Named after the German god of thunder: Thor
Greek'protos' for'ancester '. Protactinium is before Actinium in the periodic table.
Greek'ouranos' for'heaven '. Named after the planet Uranus.
Named after the planet Neptune.
Named after the planet Pluto.
Named after America.
Named after Marie Curie.
Named after the town Berkeley where it was discovered.
Named after the US-State of California.
Named after the scientist Albert Einstein.
Named after the scientist Enrico Fermi.
Named after the scientist D. I. Mendeleev.
Named after the scientist Alfred Nobel.
Named after the scientist Ernest Orlando Lawrence.
Named after the scientist Ernest Rutherford
Named after the science-town Dubna in Russia
Named after the scientist G. Theodore Seaborg.
Named after the scientist Niels Bohr.
Latin'hassia 'for the German county Hessen. In Hessen a lot elements have been discovered.
Named after the scientist Lise Meitner.
Named after the German city Darmstadt where many elements have been discovered.
Named after Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen.
Historically known as eka-mercury. Ununbium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
Historically known as eka-thallium. Ununtrium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
Historically known as eka-lead. Ununquadium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
Historically known as eka-bismuth. Ununpentium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
Historically known as eka-polonium. Ununhexium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
Temporary symbol and name. Can also be referred to as eka-astatine.
Historically known as eka-radon, eka-emanation before 1960. Ununoctium is a temporary IUPAC systematic element name.
no translations found
from the Latin'carbo 'for coal.
from the Greek'chroma 'for color, paint.
from the Latin'ferrum 'for iron.
from the Greek'xenos' for foreigner, stranger, strange.
from the Greek'astatos' for unsteady, changing
from the Greek'aktis' for ray; 'aktinoeides' for radiating.
Named after the German State (Bundesland) Hessen the Latin name of which is'Hassia '; in Hessen, many elements have been discovered.
